1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,940
All I'm saying is that if my wife, a woman that I cherish, thinks getting a job is

2
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:09,320
more important than our marriage, let her die.

3
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:12,960
Mr. Lepepki, think for just a moment.

4
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:15,740
Isn't this woman the same person you married 10 years ago?

5
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:17,420
No. She's fat.

6
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:22,760
But does she not also have a right to her own professional fulfillment?

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:24,820
Why, you whipped.

8
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:30,480
Mr. Lepepki, let me share with you a personal anecdote, if I may.

9
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:33,380
I was in a similar situation about four years ago.

10
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:35,600
Oh, your wife was putting on the pounds, too, huh?

11
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:41,680
No. No, but after 15 years as a homemaker, she suddenly decided to resume her

12
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,240
career. I couldn't believe it.

13
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,740
I mean, I certainly didn't want to give up everything I'd worked for and come home.

14
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,700
After all, I mean, the patients that you deal with in a home practice, frankly,

15
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:51,640
aren't always that interesting.

16
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:53,620
except for you.

17
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,580
So I had a decision to make.

18
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:00,700
What came first, my career or my marriage?

19
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:02,650
I came home.

20
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:05,480
And quite frankly, Mr.

21
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:07,540
Lepepki, I couldn't be happier.

22
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,520
Now, does that sound whipped to you?

23
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,420
You could put it on pumpkin pie.

24
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:17,700
All right, well, that's our session for today.

25
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:21,620
I'll be remodeling this office, so next week we'll be at another address, okay?

26
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:23,800
Okay. Oh, a real office, huh?

27
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,540
This is a real office, Mr.

28
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:26,960
Lepepki. I do real work here.

29
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:28,580
No, I didn't mean to insult you.

30
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:30,340
I welcome the change.

31
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,240
Psychiatrists with real offices are so much more interesting.

32
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:37,400
Jason, I'm home.

33
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:44,060
She could whip me anytime.

34
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:21,120
Laughter We gotta be the luckiest dreamers who never quit dreaming As long as we

35
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:30,780
keep on giving We can take anything that comes our way Baby rain or shine, all the

36
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:43,700
time We got each other sharing the laughter and love Turn it back around

37
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:57,300
Ben, my pancakes are cold.

38
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:59,100
So sit on them.

39
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:02,960
Ben, what are these little black things in my pancakes?

40
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:05,760
The ones without legs are raisinettes.

41
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:12,280
You are never gonna cook breakfast again.

42
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:13,600
That's my plan.

43
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,240
Benny, look, you have only seen the downside of Dad going to work here.

44
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,720
Now, there is an upside, too.

45
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:22,220
Okay, with Mom at work and Dad safely out of the way all week, when you get home

46
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:23,100
from school today...

47
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:26,680
You will behave exactly as if I were watching your every move.

48
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:28,200
Is that crazy?

49
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:29,200
The man read my mind.

50
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:32,100
Yeah. I brought extra diapers.

51
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:33,680
Chrissy isn't used to daycare.

52
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,060
Well, uh, where's that contractor?

53
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:38,980
I should have left five minutes ago.

54
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,180
There's all that traffic and then the toll.

55
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:45,340
Oh. Maggie, I'm gonna need an exact change for the toll.

56
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:46,960
Allow me, Dad.

57
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:50,320
Ben, give the man a buck.

58
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:54,800
Maggie, come on.

59
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:56,460
You said you'd get me some extra diapers for Chrissy.

60
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,080
I did, Jason.

61
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,180
You're nervous. Well, I'm just not a guy who's comfortable with change.

62
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:04,220
Then give me my quarters back.

63
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:07,420
You know, everything I need is right here at home.

64
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:09,740
I don't have to commute and I don't have any interruptions.

65
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:10,760
You get to watch Oprah.

66
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:13,260
Everybody knows, Dad.

67
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,700
In all these years, I thought you were doing me a big favor by working at home.

68
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:20,260
Well, I'm not the kind of guy to bring it up, but I am.

69
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:23,160
Mm -hmm. Oh, finally.

70
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:29,340
Yep. I am, uh...

71
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:36,220
I thought my truck got on the lawn.

72
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:36,940
I hope it's okay.

73
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:38,520
Yeah, everybody, this is Frank.

74
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:40,120
He's gonna be remodeling the office.

75
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:41,240
My beeper went off.

76
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:42,060
I gotta call it in.

77
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:46,120
I heard the stinkin' beeper.

78
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:47,440
Get off my back, will ya?

79
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,180
Follow me. Is any one of you named Maggie Malone?

80
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:55,800
Yes. Gil something or other from Esquire magazine.

81
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:57,860
Could you make it quick?

82
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:00,640
I heard. Your beeper went off.

83
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:03,920
Gil, can I call you right back on my husband's line?

84
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:07,340
Okay. Frank, about my office.

85
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:08,060
Just a sec.

86
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:10,260
Go ahead and talk.

87
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:11,380
I can do two things at once.

88
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:13,920
Is there anything you want to have done?

89
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:14,680
Any certain ideas?

90
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:18,580
Well, just a few random thoughts off the top of my head, really.

91
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:21,040
I... I got to let you go.

92
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:23,720
Yeah, I got another one of those.

93
00:05:24,780 --> 00:05:25,820
Hey, wait a second.

94
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,480
I'm gonna have the whole house to myself every afternoon for a whole week.

95
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:33,220
It's a good thing I've got a poker face.

96
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:38,080
Believe me, Gil, I would love to do the article, but they keep me so busy.

97
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:39,980
Losing these bookshelves will open up a whole workspace here.

98
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:42,080
Jason, I'm on the phone with Esquire.

99
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:44,960
Hey, don't we subscribe to enough magazines already?

100
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:50,740
Oh. Well, we need some time to check these plans.

101
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:53,820
Oh, Gil, I have to try yet another phone.

102
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:55,560
Be patient with me.

103
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:02,980
Sorry. So, what this office really needs is just a new face.

104
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:06,440
You know, I'd like to have an office that says, uh, professional psychiatrist works

105
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:09,580
here. A professional psychiatrist calls me home.

106
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:13,100
Dad, I was just wondering, about what time are you gonna get home tonight?

107
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:14,240
I don't know, Ben.

108
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:16,180
If I don't get out of here soon, I may not be home at all.

109
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:18,980
This is better than I thought.

110
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:22,120
What would Mike do in a situation like this?

111
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:26,300
Party, party! Pardon.

112
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:28,720
I mean, does this office say anything to you?

113
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:31,440
Not a peep.

114
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,900
Sorry. I have no time to write anything.

115
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:41,320
Bye. Oh. Can you believe I had to turn in another terrific freelance assignment?

116
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:44,180
Can you believe $3,000 for textured walls?

117
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:47,180
Sure, now that I've been offered good articles to write, I'm too busy to take them.

118
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:49,020
Not that I bother with medical school.

119
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:51,040
I find out the big bucks are in contracting.

120
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:55,540
Oh, my God.

121
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:56,520
I'm sorry, Daddy.

122
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:57,360
I wouldn't leave without you.

123
00:06:57,380 --> 00:06:57,820
I'll be good.

124
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:03,960
Oh, Christy, oh, my baby.

125
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:05,780
Oh, Christ, they can't respond.

126
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:08,580
You love me, do you?

127
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:09,660
Bye -bye, Christy.

128
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:11,960
Baby. Ruff! Ruff!

129
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:18,960
Hi. Hello. Uh, you're a little early.

130
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:20,120
The doctor's not in yet.

131
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:21,940
Oh, yes, he is.

132
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:24,140
Sure he is.

133
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:25,520
All time early.

134
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:26,740
I'm Jason Siever.

135
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:27,780
I'm gonna be in Dr.

136
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:28,800
Friedman's office. Right.

137
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:29,620
We were expecting you.

138
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:30,460
I'm Kevin Randall.

139
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:34,540
Oh, I was just putting my daughter in daycare.

140
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:37,360
So, what's your area of expertise?

141
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:39,220
Typing, filing, steno.

142
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:41,200
Oh, you treat secretaries?

143
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:43,140
I am a secretary.

144
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:44,320
In fact, I'm yours.

145
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:47,380
Ah, a male secretary.

146
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:52,340
That's kind of strangely fascinating as an idea, yes.

147
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:55,580
Um, well, I -I'll, uh, be in my office.

148
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:58,000
Which is where?

149
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:01,800
Jason Siever! You son of a gun!

150
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:03,780
Oh! How long has it been?

151
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:04,720
About a week.

152
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,400
You had dinner in my place, remember?

153
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:07,760
Oh, and why am I hugging you?

154
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:08,400
Well, I don't know.

155
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,660
You always do that when you see me, and you're starting to get all my nerves.

156
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:12,260
Hey, you seen your office?

157
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:14,080
No. Oh, I've got to show you.

158
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:19,100
Whoa. Yeah. Yeah.

159
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:24,540
Well, well, well.

160
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:26,940
All right. A male secretary?

161
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:29,200
Come on, where have you been, Jason?

162
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:32,220
Well, it's just that I've never had a secretary with hairier legs than mine.

163
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:34,660
Once, maybe, but that's another story.

164
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:38,020
I really have to thank you and Phil for letting me use this office.

165
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:40,060
Oh, geez, hey, please, don't mention Phil, huh?

166
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,820
He's off at a Zorak conference, and I'm here stuck with a double patient load.

167
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:46,000
Oh, well, at least you're not bitter about it.

168
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:48,880
Nice furniture, huh?

169
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:49,540
Are you kidding?

170
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:53,500
I own it, but I lease it back to myself through my own corporation for a double

171
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,860
write -off. You still practice psychiatry, though?

172
00:08:56,980 --> 00:08:57,980
Nice chair, huh?

173
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:00,260
Hey. It vibrates.

174
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:01,600
Oh, good. I thought it was me.

175
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,680
And it's got a built -in phone.

176
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:06,820
No. Yeah. Okay, let's see now.

177
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,300
You know about the daycare center?

178
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:09,940
You know there's a running track on the roof here?

179
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:11,000
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

180
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:11,620
I gotta get going.

181
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,500
I've got two patients waiting for me.

182
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:16,480
All right. Well, if you get swamped this week, I'll be happy to pitch in.

183
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:18,700
Oh, I may take you up on that.

184
00:09:18,780 --> 00:09:20,540
All right, I'll just have your chair call my chair.

185
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:21,980
Ha ha ha.

186
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:32,800
Hello? Hello? He has got to be kidding.

187
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,420
Thelma, have you seen this assignment sheet?

188
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,960
Nope. Thelma, you typed it.

189
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:39,240
Well, yeah, but I didn't read it.

190
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:40,660
That way I don't get involved.

191
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:43,800
I'm supposed to do a feature entitled,

192
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:45,780
Winterizing Your Dog.

193
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:48,320
So rather than writing an insightful,

194
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:52,680
respectable, journalistic piece for Esquire magazine, I'll be showing people how to

195
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:54,480
put snow tires on their shih tzus.

196
00:09:57,740 --> 00:09:58,620
Nice alliteration.

197
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,640
So this is the day I take my stand.

198
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:07,560
Mr. Sidlovich, I am a darn good reporter, and I have worked very hard to show you

199
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:11,320
that, but it's not easy when the biggest story I've done in months is gingivitis or

200
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:12,960
your gums trying to tell you something.

201
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:18,860
I have failed to make use of your journalistic talent.

202
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:20,820
I accept full responsibility.

203
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,020
As of today, I'm out of here.

204
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:25,920
Oh, well, I didn't mean that you had to quit.

205
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:27,000
I didn't quit.

206
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,720
I'm fired. You're kidding.

207
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:29,860
No, I'm fired.

208
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:33,660
If I were kidding, I would not be packing all of my worldly possessions into this

209
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:34,920
pathetically small box.

210
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:38,560
You want my happy feet?

211
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:43,140
Mr. Sidlovich, how can we do this to you?

212
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:45,100
Station manager's unhappy with our image.

213
00:10:45,820 --> 00:10:47,420
So we hired a media consultant.

214
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:49,660
They consulted. I'm gone.

215
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:53,780
But that is so unfair!

216
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:56,340
Oh, what if we're all gone?

217
00:10:56,680 --> 00:10:58,140
Oh, I don't think you have to worry, Maggie.

218
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:00,460
I said some very nice things about you.

219
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:03,840
Of course, I said some very nice things about me, too, and look what happened.

220
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:07,880
Well, I guess that's about it.

221
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:12,940
Mr. Sidlovich, if there's anything I can do, let me know.

222
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:13,980
I mean it.

223
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:19,880
Anything. You know, I might take a shot at print journalism again.

224
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:22,980
That guy from Esquire that keeps calling you?

225
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:23,820
What's his number?

226
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:26,480
Oh, it's, uh, 565.

227
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:29,120
Wait a second.

228
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:31,740
Mr. Sidlovich, I may need that number.

229
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:36,160
Bye, Maggie. Bye, Sid.

230
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:45,760
Bye, office. No number, no feet.

231
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:53,920
Oh, Mom, great.

232
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:54,720
I'm glad you're home.

233
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:56,220
Oh, you are?

234
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:59,320
Oh, yeah. I forgot Dad was at work and I thought I'd have to make my own dinner.

235
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:02,300
Good to see you too, Mike.

236
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:06,100
Oh, Mom, you've had a hard day at work, huh?

237
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:08,380
Hey, listen, you know, you should really ease up.

238
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:10,640
I mean, you're getting some major frown lines.

239
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:14,320
If you value your life, Mike,

240
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:19,400
back off. Hiya, Maggie.

241
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:22,900
Hello, Mike. Dad, get a grip on yourself.

242
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:26,360
Well, you certainly seem to have had a good day.

243
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:28,740
Oh, good doesn't begin to describe it, Maggie.

244
00:12:28,740 --> 00:12:29,760
I had a great day.

245
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:31,840
I'm telling you, I have the most fabulous office.

246
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:33,020
The people down there are terrific.

247
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:38,100
And today, two, count them, two doctors consulted me I have never been so excited.

248
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:39,920
How was your day?

249
00:12:40,840 --> 00:12:42,160
My boss was fired.

250
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:44,080
And I may be next.

251
00:12:52,100 --> 00:12:54,980
Oh, Jason, it's like a morgue down at the station.

252
00:12:55,420 --> 00:12:58,920
They hired a media consultant to change our whole image, and today he was deciding

253
00:12:58,920 --> 00:12:59,680
who to can.

254
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,020
Oh, sweetheart, just because you're being evaluated doesn't mean you're gonna be

255
00:13:03,020 --> 00:13:05,000
fired. You're a good reporter.

